🚀 From Fresh VM to Automated DevOps Lab with Ansible & Docker

Recently, I built my own 3-Node DevOps Lab to understand automation at a deeper level using Ansible and Docker.

Here’s a summary of my journey and key learnings.

🔹 What is Ansible?

Ansible is an open-source automation tool used for:

Configuration Management

Application Deployment

Infrastructure Automation

It works in an agentless manner using SSH and simple YAML playbooks.

🔹 Why Ansible?

✔ Eliminates repetitive manual work
✔ Ensures environment consistency
✔ Reduces deployment errors
✔ Enables Infrastructure as Code
✔ Perfect for DevOps automation

🔹 Where & When Do We Use It?

Provisioning cloud servers

Deploying applications

Installing packages (like Nginx, Docker, etc.)

Managing multi-node environments

CI/CD integrations

In real projects, Ansible works alongside Git, CI/CD tools, container platforms, and monitoring systems.


🔹 How I Built My 3-Node DevOps Lab (Step-by-Step)
✅ Step 1: Installed Docker on VM
apt-get update
curl -fsSL https://get.docker.com -o install-docker.sh
sh install-docker.sh
docker --version

✅ Step 2: Created 3-Node Environment Using Containers

ansible_master (Control Node)

target1

target2

docker run -itd --name ansible_master ubuntu /bin/bash
docker run -itd --name target1 ubuntu /bin/bash
docker run -itd --name target2 ubuntu /bin/bash


This simulated a real infrastructure setup.

✅ Step 3: Verified Containers
docker ps
docker inspect target1


Inspection helped understand networking, metadata, and container IP allocation — critical for automation.

✅ Step 4: Installed Ansible on Control Node

Inside ansible_master:

apt install ansible
ansible --version


Configured inventory:

vi /etc/ansible/hosts


Generated SSH keys & connected to target:

ssh-keygen
ssh-copy-id root@<target-ip>


Verified connectivity:

ssh root@<target-ip>

✅ Step 5: Automated Nginx Installation

Created playbook:

vi installnginx.yaml


Executed:

ansible-playbook installnginx.yaml


Nginx was installed automatically on target node — no manual login required.

This demonstrated:

✔ Idempotency
✔ Centralized automation
✔ Real-world configuration management

✅ Step 6: Created Custom Docker Images

After configuration:

docker commit target1 rahulnegi17/target1:v1
docker commit ansible_master rahulnegi17/ansible_master:v1


Why this matters?

Environment consistency

Version control

Reproducibility

✅ Step 7: Pushed Images to Docker Hub
docker login
docker push rahulnegi17/ansible_master:v1
docker push rahulnegi17/target1:v1


Now images are globally accessible.

✅ Step 8: Tested Portability
docker image rm rahulnegi17/ansible_master:v1
docker pull rahulnegi17/ansible_master:v1


Confirmed:

✔ Image versioning works
✔ Registry integration works
✔ Infrastructure is portable

🔥 Key DevOps Takeaways

Containers are perfect for lab simulations

Versioned images improve deployment reliability

Automation reduces human error

Infrastructure becomes reproducible

Hands-on labs build real confidence

💡 Why This Matters in Real Projects

In production environments:

Teams build custom images

Push to registries

Use them in CI/CD pipelines

Deploy consistently across staging & production

Ansible acts as the automation engine connecting everything.
